Phoenix-RTOS: KMSDRM scans out XRGB8888 instead of ARGB8888.

rpi4-kms stacks the primary plane above the firmware console framebuffer and
passes ARGB8888 to the firmware as a per-pixel-alpha layer, so a GL game that
leaves alpha < 1 in its back buffer (glClear alpha 0, blended passes) could
show the console text through the picture. SDL_EGL_SetRequiredVisualId()
takes the same variable, so the EGLConfig becomes the XRGB8888 one Mesa's GBM
platform also offers (kmscube's default). Phoenix-only.

--- a/src/video/kmsdrm/SDL_kmsdrmvideo.c
+++ b/src/video/kmsdrm/SDL_kmsdrmvideo.c
@@ -1195,7 +1195,16 @@
     SDL_VideoDisplay *display = SDL_GetDisplayForWindow(window);
     SDL_DisplayData *dispdata = (SDL_DisplayData *)display->driverdata;
 
+#ifdef __phoenix__
+    /* rpi4-kms stacks the primary plane ABOVE the firmware console framebuffer
+       and hands ARGB8888 to the firmware as a blended (per-pixel alpha) layer,
+       so an application that leaves alpha < 1 in its back buffer (most GL games
+       do) could show the console through the picture. Scan out an opaque
+       format instead (also what kmscube uses). */
+    uint32_t surface_fmt = GBM_FORMAT_XRGB8888;
+#else
     uint32_t surface_fmt = GBM_FORMAT_ARGB8888;
+#endif
     uint32_t surface_flags = GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT | GBM_BO_USE_RENDERING;
 
     EGLContext egl_context;
